opengwasdb resolve-analyses supports source_reader_capability=opengwasdb.gwas-vcf through the same bounded resolver API as GWAS-SSF. For each Analysis it performs one streaming bcftools query over source records, after a header capability probe, and produces ancestry and phenotype-SD evidence without reopening the records for reference-AF fallback.


Every manifest row must provide source_genome_build. source_assembly is a compatibility alias; if both are populated, their normalized builds must agree. --source-build is an explicit fallback only for rows that omit both. The ancestry reference build is always supplied with --reference-build.
Same-build inputs proceed without transformation. A GRCh37/hg19 GWAS-VCF used with a GRCh38/hg38 reference requires --chain-file. Each lifted SNP must have one mapping; a reverse-strand mapping complements both source alleles before canonical orientation, so AF and signed effects still name the transformed effect allele. Ambiguous mappings and indels (whose target alleles a single position lift cannot establish) fail rather than choosing or inventing Variant Identity. Other build directions and cross-build Source Formats without an implemented liftover projection produce a controlled failure before source records are read. Builds are not inferred from filenames, coordinates, or VCF headers.
Source build, reference build, chain path, chain SHA-256, chain size and chain mtime are stored in each resolution record and its resume fingerprint. Source identity continues to use the manifest-recorded checksum together with the observed file size and mtime; the resolver does not hash every source body.



The VCF projection reads one sample’s FORMAT fields:

EZ is the preferred z score when finite;
otherwise a usable ES / SE supplies z;
resolver beta is exactly z * SE;
AF is the ALT/effect-allele frequency and is reoriented with the allele;
SE must be finite and positive.

The existing ancestry rule is unchanged: a canonical, non-palindromic row needs usable AF and SE, but not an effect. Phenotype-SD evidence retains the existing association eligibility, so a missing effect excludes that row from SD evidence without excluding an otherwise usable ancestry row. A missing SE excludes the row from ancestry evidence too. Multiallelic and unnormalisable identities keep the existing reader exclusions.
The reader holds at most one metrics chunk (50,000 rows by default); ancestry and SD evidence remain bounded by the resolver’s extraction panel and evidence sample. A non-zero bcftools exit or structurally malformed query output is a controlled per-Analysis failure, never a successful short EOF. When a resolver bound closes a still-running query intentionally, cleanup terminates it, waits for at most five seconds, then kills and bounded-waits again. A process already known to have failed is never treated as that intentional cancellation.



GWAS-VCF resolution does not support a declared INFO/imputation-score filter. The manifest parser refuses that configuration rather than silently ignoring it. The path does not choose method tiers, alter ancestry gates or formulas, decide Store membership, or execute a Store build. Those remain caller-owned policy as described by ADR 0044.

